WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
As the Betancourt family heads&nbsp;
out to a party on Purge night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.600
you may notice a sign in their front yard,&nbsp;
noting that the house is armed by Sandin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.520
Security. This little detail is one of many in&nbsp;
The Purge series that references James Sandin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.360
from the original movie. You may recall that Mr.&nbsp;
Sandin used his own company’s security system to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.120
protect his family, and that didn’t work out too&nbsp;
well for him, and he was purged before morning.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.360
Despite the supposed poor&nbsp;
quality of these systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.760
they aren’t the only ones&nbsp;
to trust the Sandin brand.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.120
DAVID: “State of the art Sandin Security.”

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.160
JOE: “Who’s providing the shell system?”
RICK: “Sandin.”&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.840
JOE: “The best.”

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.840
Even the NFFA once trusted Sandin to&nbsp;
install their system. It’s surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.440
that Sandin is still trusted in The Purge&nbsp;
Season 1. According to the NFFA website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.680
their main competitor, G&amp;D security, overtook&nbsp;
them after James Sandin’s passing, stating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.760
“we’re what Sandin promised to be, and wasn’t.”

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.040
The Purge Season 1 takes place in&nbsp;
2027 based on the dates displayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.080
on these security systems. We can assume&nbsp;
Henry’s signature on this release form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.840
was a typo. That sets the season 5 years&nbsp;
after Mr. Sandin’s system was breached,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.800
so I’m guessing improvements were&nbsp;
made to the company after his passing.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.680
To learn all of the hidden connections in&nbsp;
Season 1 and Season 2 of The Purge TV series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.840
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.240
I finally found a Purge mask that not&nbsp;
only doesn’t block my speech volume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.160
but also displays the goatee for the&nbsp;
world to see in all of its glory.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.000
Welcome to Things You Missed. If you can’t&nbsp;
get enough Purge lore from the movies, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.840
is another 13 hours of content to consider, and&nbsp;
it’s called The Purge. Again. Let’s get into it.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.120
The series opens with this shot of some&nbsp;
blue and yellow shower loofahs. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.400
apparently these things are called loofahs.&nbsp;
We later find out that these colors represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.080
the robe colors of an organization called&nbsp;
the Mercy cult. These kids are manipulated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.560
into giving themselves up to the&nbsp;
Purge. Once they are old enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.600
they graduate from these yellow robes&nbsp;
to the light blue ones that they die in.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.000
Light blue has always been associated with the&nbsp;
NFFA and The Purge, but it’s not until Season 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.680
that we discover the meaning of yellow.&nbsp;
Yellow is the color of Remembrance Day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.760
the holiday that takes place 3 months later&nbsp;
to commemorate the lives lost on Purge night.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.480
In the first episode, Miguel Guerrero&nbsp;
is looking for his sister at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.600
abandoned structure. This is actually&nbsp;
an existing location in New Orleans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.040
but the blue flowers and grim reaper&nbsp;
happen to work perfectly with the Purge.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.880
As you know, the dystopian government party in&nbsp;
The Purge franchise is called The New Founding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.280
Fathers of America, and the series features many&nbsp;
locations and references to America’s original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.000
founding fathers who helped America gain its&nbsp;
independence in 1776. The finale of Season 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.200
takes place at Thomas Paine High School,&nbsp;
which comes up several times in the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.600
The Stanton family lives on Jefferson St, named&nbsp;
after Thomas Jefferson. In Jane’s flashback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.280
we see that she attended Thomas Jefferson High&nbsp;
School. There’s also a street called Franklin.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.560
“We have roadblocks,&nbsp;
[unintelligable], go across Franklin.”

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.200
...named after Ben Franklin. The classroom&nbsp;
seen in Episode 9 shows the constitutional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.720
amendments and many of the Founding Fathers.&nbsp;
When Miguel and Pete search for the school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.320
they come across a map of Washington&nbsp;
Street, named after George Washington.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.160
Likewise, in Season 2, Esme Carmona searches&nbsp;
Professor Adams’s house and there’s a framed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.400
picture of the Lincoln Memorial, a location&nbsp;
also seen in The Purge: Election Year. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.720
in the last episode of Season 2, reports go out&nbsp;
about illegal use of weapons in Washington Square,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.720
another reference to George Washington.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.520
If you’ve seen my previous Purge Things&nbsp;
You Missed episodes, which you should have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.840
you know all about the name Sabian. Every&nbsp;
Purge installment has a character named Sabian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.720
because they’re all written by James&nbsp;
Demonaco, whose trademark is to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.840
that name in his writing. In the series,&nbsp;
Sabian is mentioned in the first episode.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.280
JENNA: “Oh and don’t forget, after you close&nbsp;
the deal, he’s probably gonna make a pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.560
for us to become NFFA party members.”
RICK: “I know, I know, I’m ready for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.280
I’ll deflect my way out of it. Now remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.800
Billy Sabian closed a huge deal for his&nbsp;
start up here last year and he didn’t join.”

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.880
Another element we’ve come to expect in all Purge&nbsp;
entries is the inclusion of civil rights leaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.720
in the sets, as a comparison between America’s&nbsp;
Civil Rights movement and the mistreatment of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.720
poor in the world of The Purge. This time, you can spot&nbsp;
it in the hideout of Henry’s gang, where you’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.440
see images of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. and other&nbsp;
figures, as well as a quote by Harriet Tubman.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.488
MLK and Malcolm X are quoted on the wall as well.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.680
Another aspect we’ve become very familiar&nbsp;
with is the idea that each Purge is bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.320
than the last. There are two Purges shown in&nbsp;
each season. First there’s the 2027 Purge.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.360
“Prognosticators are predicting&nbsp;a 
record turnout tonight.”

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.840
We only see about 90 seconds of the 2028 Purge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.080
so participation is not mentioned, but&nbsp;
the 2036 Purge continues the trend.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.880
“Estimates came in already. Thirty percent&nbsp;
uptick from last year in every district.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.240
Biggest Purge in city history.”

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.240
The 2037 Purge is no exception.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.360
VIVIAN: “You ever seen crowds this big?”
CURTIS: “Never. This years tracking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.040
become the largest Purge we’ve seen.”

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.400
One of the big Season 1 plotlines&nbsp;
has to do with Jenna being pregnant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.080
There are a couple signs early on, like her&nbsp;
asking for Tylenol and ordering salt-water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.400
instead of alcohol. That being said, I still&nbsp;
find this to be kind of a risky question.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.800
CATALINA: “How far along?”
JENNA: “Is it that obvious?”

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.520
The second episode would be filled&nbsp;
with more connections to the movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.880
as well as easter eggs connecting&nbsp;
it to other horror movie franchises.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.680
In Miguel’s quest to find his sister, he&nbsp;
comes across a Triage Van, a concept we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.960
saw more of further down the timeline in&nbsp;
The Purge: Election Year. There are a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.160
Election Year references in the series,&nbsp;
like when Miguel figures out his sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.913
is at the Carnival of Flesh, goes there and&nbsp;
hears this phrase chanted by the announcer.

00:05:05.913 --> 00:05:10.320
“Purge and Purify! Purge and&nbsp;
Purify! Purge and Purify!”

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.880
That’s the same motto chanted by Edwidge Owens,&nbsp;
the main antagonist of Election Year. In Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.240
there’s a cold open set in a company&nbsp;
meeting for The Merrymakers Mask Company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.440
and we see their logo strongly resembles&nbsp;
the style of masks used by the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.160
tourists in Election Year. We also see that Purge&nbsp;
Tourism trend in another cold open in Season 2.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.440
Later on in Season 2, there’s also a part&nbsp;
where a van gets flipped over. I don’t know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.040
if it’s a reference, but we’ve definitely&nbsp;
seen it all before in Election Year.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.560
One of the trials Miguel has to overcome to get&nbsp;
to Penelope is an obstacle course known as The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.480
Gauntlet. If the name sounds familiar, you may&nbsp;
be thinking of the 2016 Halloween Horror Nights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:43.920
maze The Purge: Gauntlet of Fear, which&nbsp;
happened two years before the Gauntlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.280
appeared in the show. Other than the fact&nbsp;
that there’s actual danger in this Gauntlet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.160
it really does feel a lot like&nbsp;
a Halloween Horror Nights maze.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.160
I would definitely be the camera guy wearing skull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.760
makeup, and this is actually the stunt&nbsp;
co-ordinator of the show making a cameo.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.400
There are actually a lot of funny&nbsp;
Purge groups in this season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:02.000
my favorite of which is these guys who dress&nbsp;
up as golfers for some reason. And when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.880
take their victim away, I love how he does&nbsp;
this giddy-up motion. Imagine being that guy.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.320
The Purge is produced by Blumhouse,&nbsp;
who has always been known to put funny stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.520
into some of their horror movies. One&nbsp;
example of this is Happy Death Day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.280
which is referenced in the entrance area to&nbsp;
Pete’s Cantina. Masks are checked at the door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.040
and on the rack, you can spot the Baby mask&nbsp;
worn by the antagonist in Happy Death Day.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.160
The property is referenced again in&nbsp;
Season 2, where college students Ben&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.840
and Turner live in a frat house that seems&nbsp;
to be the same as the sorority house from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:31.280
the Happy Death Day movies. I believe the&nbsp;
campus they attend is the same as well.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.040
It looks like Ben’s dorm room even has a&nbsp;
poster for a similar mascot. In Happy Death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.360
Day it was the Bayfield Babies, this poster&nbsp;
is for a team called New Orleans Baby Cakes.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.720
There are plenty of other references on the&nbsp;
wall of masks throughout the night though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.200
like the sheep mask and fox mask, from the 2011&nbsp;
movie, You’re Next, a mask that was worn by one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:51.200
of the Mercy Cult purgers in episode 1, the&nbsp;
mask of Godhead, from The Purge: Anarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.040
(more on that later). The mask shown on the&nbsp;
sign appears to be the same mask worn by Polite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.680
Stranger in the original Purge movie, and that&nbsp;
mask actually appears on the rack in Episode 8.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.480
It’s not just the movies that are&nbsp;
referenced though. This may be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.280
Finders Keepers app mentioned on the NFFA website.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.800
Of course, I can’t leave out The First&nbsp;
Purge. I mean, I could, it’s my channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.320
but I’m not going to. One of the storylines&nbsp;
follows a businesswoman named Jane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:15.200
who looks out her window and sees the&nbsp;
only building lit up on Purge night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:18.400
is a bank. This is part of the idea&nbsp;
that The Purge is fueled by money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.400
Jane works at an equity firm, and in a flashback&nbsp;
to her job interview, she says the following.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:28.800
JANE: “I’ve been following your firm since I read&nbsp;
a profile in Forbes back when I was an undergrad.”

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.520
This is the second time Forbes&nbsp;
has come in this franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:35.040
the first being on a police officer’s&nbsp;
name badge in The First Purge. Side note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.040
in Jane’s flashback we see her boss checking&nbsp;
her out as she leaves… but she never sees him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.680
so how does she have that in her&nbsp;
memory. It doesn’t make sense.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:45.120
There’s another flashback in the following&nbsp;
episode, where Miguel and Penelope’s parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.800
are offered money to stay home during The Purge.&nbsp;
We can see the pamphlet that says Staten Island

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:52.560
experiment, this is the same pamphlet&nbsp;
we saw being handed out in The First Purge.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.080
The First Purge isn’t the only movie&nbsp;
to have this struggle of classes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.720
Many of the entries make it obvious that the NFFA&nbsp;
is power because they are rich and The Purge is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.960
all about making money. There’s actually a piece&nbsp;
of graffiti in New Orleans that says 2 MUCH THREL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:10.160
which is a slang term for a rich person, so this&nbsp;
is likely a form of propaganda against the NFFA.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.640
We see how their brainwashing of&nbsp;
the American people has affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:15.600
people when Jane’s employee Alison&nbsp;
purges her co-worker in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.280
ensure a promotion. I’m not sure&nbsp;
I understand the logic here. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.360
murder is legal on this night, but wouldn’t&nbsp;
she still be fired for offing a co-worker?

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:27.200
Anyway, Penelope is taken away by a group&nbsp;
of purgers wearing nun outfits. Not sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.240
if this is a thematic choice, given&nbsp;
how some groups are starting to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.000
purging as a religious act, thanks to&nbsp;
NFFA propaganda, or just a result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.840
this show coming out in 2018 and creepy&nbsp;
nuns being really popular at the time.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.120
What’s equally unclear is if the&nbsp;
anti-NFFA checkpoints set up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:45.120
the streets are related to the anti-NFFA&nbsp;
group from Anarchy. All I can confirm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.720
is that a couple of their members seem to&nbsp;
be wearing berets, as some of the Anarchy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.880
resistance did. This headline seen at the end of&nbsp;
Season 1 is also familiar if you’ve seen Anarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.160
it warns of ‘Heavy Consequences&nbsp;
for Purge Night “Revolutionaries”’.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.520
Oh, here’s another funny part.

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.480
“Wah haha hah.”

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.160
This is part of the aforementioned Carnival of&nbsp;
Flesh, which is also where you’ll find probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:08.800
the most hilarious mask in all of Purge. But the&nbsp;
Carnival may have more sinister affiliations than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:14.080
you might have realized. The “O” in the logo is&nbsp;
a Celtic Cross turned 45°. This is a symbol used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.160
by Neo-Nazis and Ku Klux Klan members. It’s&nbsp;
also displayed in the merch cart inside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:23.200
letter “O” in shopping. The carnival also sells&nbsp;
merchandise with KKK depictions. These racist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.200
groups have been known to work with the NFFA&nbsp;
and take advantage of The Purge in the movies.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.440
Penelope spends like three to four episodes&nbsp;
captive at the carnival thanks to her vengeful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:35.600
ex-boyfriend Henry. Meanwhile, Rick and Jenna&nbsp;
escape after the Purge party they are attending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.760
is attacked by revolutionaries. Then they get&nbsp;
held at gunpoint at home, by their neighbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.440
Ross. This part actually drives me crazy.&nbsp;
It’s as if they don’t understand that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.960
can be conflict in a story without&nbsp;
someone holding someone at gunpoint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:50.640
Especially since we just saw like&nbsp;
3-4 episodes of that kind of tension.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.280
And then if you’re going to do&nbsp;
it AGAIN almost immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.760
at least make it make sense -- like&nbsp;
she’s about to shoot this dude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.960
and the people standing behind him aren’t&nbsp;
even gonna move? What if she misses? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.800
if she doesn’t but she blows his brains into the&nbsp;
crowd? Or the bullet could go right through him.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.480
Now before I move onto Season 2, there are&nbsp;
a couple things present in the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:09.463
all throughout Season 1 that I’ve yet to touch on.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:24.000
What if I put the Plus-head mask on over the Angry&nbsp;
Abraham Lincoln mask… That’s kinda not bad!

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.160
The New Founding Fathers of America have&nbsp;
one practice that the originals never would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.520
have been able to: using technology&nbsp;
for surveillance of the citizens.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.200
JANE: “I forgot that you were&nbsp;
the eavesdropping type, David.”&nbsp;

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.640
DAVID: “You know I’m always&nbsp;
watching and listening Jane.”

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:40.720
We saw this more than ever in the previous entry&nbsp;
-- The First Purge, where I pointed out the eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.040
that symbolize the always watching government.&nbsp;
The TV series gives us way more of that.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:47.520
In the Betancourts’ house, you’ll&nbsp;
see large artwork in the hallway of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.240
a human eye. When they return&nbsp;
home safely after the party I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.960
noticed another eye up above a&nbsp;
painting near the front door.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.040
In Henry’s apartment during a flashback, he’s&nbsp;
also got artwork of an eye, a very illuminati&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:00.560
looking one at that. An interesting note&nbsp;
here is that it says the word: Purity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.680
which may go along with that often&nbsp;
repeated phrase: Purge and Purify.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:06.320
JOE: “I can Purge and Purify myself.”

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:09.440
Another note on this piece is that this&nbsp;
eye actually has a sword going into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:12.240
a possible foreshadowing of what&nbsp;
happens to Henry -- his cornea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.400
is permanently damaged in a fight with Miguel.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.680
Jane’s Purge night is a crazy&nbsp;
one with many destinations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:20.640
one of which is a sexist human&nbsp;
art gallery held by her boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:24.960
David Ryker. He too is the proud owner&nbsp;
of some artwork of a big human eye.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.240
At the end of Season 1, everyone is&nbsp;
brought together by the main antagonist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.720
Joe Owens. Joe has been saving each of the main&nbsp;
characters, because he wants to put them on trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:36.160
where they can either give a worthy&nbsp;
confession, or be purged by Joe himself.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:40.880
Joe is very much like Jigsaw, specifically,&nbsp;
from the movie 2017 self-titled movie. Both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:44.000
characters go around capturing people who&nbsp;
have wronged them, in order to try to force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.400
them to confess to their wrongdoing. Also,&nbsp;
Eleanor is here, so, there’s that. Joe plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:52.080
his victims’ abductions by posting all&nbsp;
the information he has onto a big wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.240
something we’ve seen in the Saw franchise in the&nbsp;
past, we learn he’s extremely smart and good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:00.640
at math in a flashback and he even builds traps&nbsp;
that try to account for every possible outcome.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:04.720
They even share the similarity of having&nbsp;
basic, non-suspicious American names.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.840
“Oh my God, Joe?”
“Joe Owens?!”&nbsp;

00:12:11.947 --> 00:12:16.542
“John? John Kramer?!”

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.360
Anyway, the reason I bring up Joe Owens with the&nbsp;
eye motif, is because he too ends up getting an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:25.280
eye poked out. When it comes to Joe though, I’m&nbsp;
not entirely sure that he’s dead. In the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.280
battle, Miguel shoots his bulletproof vest,&nbsp;
shoots his knee and kicks him into the pool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:33.600
We never get confirmation that Joe died, and&nbsp;
I’ll circle back to that later in the video.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.680
The last thing I have to hit&nbsp;
on before fast forwarding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:38.880
Season 2 is our crew reference easter&nbsp;
eggs. If you’re new to this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:44.080
filmmakers love to hide their own names in movies&nbsp;
and apparently TV because we’re very egotistical.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:48.480
It starts at the rehab center Penelope discharged&nbsp;
from: Kantrow Rehabilitation Center is named after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:52.400
producer Alissa Kantrow. There’s a list of&nbsp;
visitors at that rehab center that includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:57.200
first AD Paul Uddo and production accountant&nbsp;
Jeff Reynolds. The character Rick Betancourt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:01.760
is named after co-executive producer and writer&nbsp;
Mick Betancourt. Pete’s Cantina has a poster for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:06.880
a jazz show by Neal Orillion, which happens to&nbsp;
be similar to the name of the art director, Nealy Orillion.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:12.000
David Ryker shows off an art piece that he calls&nbsp;
“an original Lemercier”. This is probably a reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.640
executive producer Sébastien K. Lemercier.&nbsp;
During one of Joe’s trials in Episode 9, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:20.480
tries to get a confession from someone who picked&nbsp;
on him in high school, and shows a yearbook page.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:24.480
Most of the other student names on this page are&nbsp;
crew. You can pause if you want to read them all.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:28.160
Finally, there’s a flashback to the moment when&nbsp;
Rick and Joe had their falling out. The words:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:30.560
McNight House are written on the&nbsp;
whiteboard in the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.200
that’s a nod to cinematographer Kevin McNight).

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:37.120
The motif of an eye as a symbol for government&nbsp;
surveillance would continue in Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:40.400
and there would be a lot more examples&nbsp;
-- almost as many as the amount of times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:43.120
a character expresses their&nbsp;
dislike for this damn night!&nbsp;

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:46.480
“I’m gonna try to sleep through&nbsp;
this God-forsaken holiday.”&nbsp;

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.480
“I hate this night.”
“Must be the night.”&nbsp;

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:51.280
“It’s nice to officially meet&nbsp;
you on this sh**ty night.”&nbsp;

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:54.640
“I hate this night!”
“This night corrupts everyone.”&nbsp;

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:57.920
“This is their way of protesting this damn night.”
“C’mon, let’s go find a place to wait out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:00.720
rest of this f***ing night.”
“I just hate this night.”&nbsp;

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:05.360
“God, this night. This night is…”
“She knows what night it is.”&nbsp;

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:08.320
“This night has been a long one.”
“I think this night has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:11.200
taken a toll on all of us.”
“Maybe he’s just having a bad night.”&nbsp;

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:14.160
“...but this night is relentless.”
“I hate this night.”&nbsp;

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:18.160
“This night just kind of makes me start&nbsp;
thinking about what’s really important.”&nbsp;

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:21.600
“A baby would probably make&nbsp;
this night even harder.”&nbsp;

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:25.760
“She hated this holiday, she wouldn’t be outside.”
“I’m done with this night.”&nbsp;

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:28.880
“God I hate this holiday.”
“I hate Purge night.”

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:32.000
The first eye motif can be found on the&nbsp;
necklace of Ben Gardner’s girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:35.920
Kellen. There are many instances of eyes being&nbsp;
present in the graffiti found around the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:40.000
whether it’s a random piece of plywood, on the&nbsp;
bank, on a warehouse accompanied by the text:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.000
‘I’m watchin you’, or in an alley. The presence&nbsp;
of these eyes play an even more important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.760
role this season, where the plot literally&nbsp;
revolves around the NFFA surveillance team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:52.080
and areas with no cameras known as “dead zones''&nbsp;
are very important for the main characters.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.440
The surveillance team is known as the&nbsp;
NFFA’s visual analytics department,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:58.200
but most people have a different name for them.

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:01.440
RYAN: “You’re government.”
ESME: “Yes. I am.”&nbsp;

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:04.640
RYAN: “Which department?”
ESME: “Visual analytics.”&nbsp;

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:05.680
RYAN: “Eyes in the sky.”

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:09.840
Their office has computers with an animated&nbsp;
desktop background. The logo says: New Founding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:14.640
Fathers of America -- established 1984. Of&nbsp;
course, they weren’t really established in 1984,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.640
they were established in 2014, which you know if&nbsp;
you’ve seen my Horror History episode on them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:23.600
Later episodes do not contain this text. The 1984&nbsp;
is a reference to the novel, Nineteen Eighty-Four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:27.600
by George Orwell, which is about a dystopian&nbsp;
future government who controls the population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:33.043
through constant surveillance, much like the&nbsp;
NFFA, who always have eyes on their citizens.

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:45.280
In the second episode of Season 2, Ben&nbsp;
relieves his urge 2 Purge with The Purge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:49.760
video game in a kiosk… in the mall. He exits&nbsp;
when another guy complains about how long he’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:53.440
spent in there and sees that he’s wearing&nbsp;
a shirt with three pairs of gorillas’ eyes.

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:56.080
Esme’s sister has a poster of&nbsp;
a peacock feather in her room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:59.200
which kind of looks like an eye, although&nbsp;
maybe she’s just a Jimmy Eat World fan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:02.000
Esme has a pillow in her own&nbsp;
home with a giant eye on it.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:05.040
The opening of episode 8 shows&nbsp;
off a mask with one eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:08.160
perhaps a callback to Henry from&nbsp;
Season 1. In the frat house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:12.240
there is a beer pong rules board which also,&nbsp;
for some reason, contains a pair of eyes.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:16.160
The main antagonist of Season 2 is Ben,&nbsp;
who loses his mind after nearly getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:20.080
purged by a man in a Godhead mask. Many of&nbsp;
you wondered in my Anarchy video if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:23.760
is the same Godhead from Anarchy. That&nbsp;
guy was black, and this guy is white,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:28.320
so I’m gonna say no, they’re not the&nbsp;
same guy, but I guess creating this mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:32.480
became somewhat of a Purge tradition, as&nbsp;
you’ll recall, we also saw one in Season 1.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:35.440
Maybe the NFFA emblem posted&nbsp;
next to his bed is a clue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:38.640
he’s going to become evil. I’m not sure&nbsp;
why the Big Brain meme is here though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:42.480
maybe it has something to do with Ben being an&nbsp;
example of the findings of Prof. Adams’s study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:45.520
that aggression on Purge increases&nbsp;
aggression throughout the rest of the year.

00:16:47.041 --> 00:16:52.754
“So those brain scans... they show that violent acts can light up the pleasure centers of the brain like a drug.

00:16:52.754 --> 00:16:56.387
Especially those in the younger generation, those who grew up with the 28th ammendment.” 

00:16:56.387 --> 00:17:00.640
Bobby listens to an audio program from Bobby&nbsp;
Sheridan, the same speaker who inspired Joe Owens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:05.280
in Season 1. At one point, he listens to the same&nbsp;
recording that Joe had on in the Season 1 finale.

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:11.920
BOBBY: “People will always surprise you when they&nbsp;
don’t want to die. But when you embrace the Purge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:18.960
When you commit to the healing power&nbsp;
of it’s violence. Anything can happen.”

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:23.840
Ben starts taking out victims off-purge, the first&nbsp;
of which is a farmer, who is eventually found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:28.160
but the cause of death is reported as heat stroke.&nbsp;
I was having a little bit of trouble understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:32.240
why the NFFA would lie about this. It could be&nbsp;
part of their plan to give people the impression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:37.520
that The Purge is working, that crime during the&nbsp;
other 364 days a year is down. But then why be so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:42.240
strict about people whose crimes went past 7AM?&nbsp;
Maybe they only want to admit crimes occur when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:46.080
they think they can catch the person in order to&nbsp;
make it look like nearly all criminals are caught,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.600
and since Ben took out the farmer in the&nbsp;
countryside, there were no cameras on it.

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:53.840
This could also explain why they let him off the&nbsp;
hook after he admits to smoking marijuana. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:58.320
public doesn’t know that a crime occured, it’s in&nbsp;
the NFFA’s best interest to keep it that way. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:02.560
I don’t understand is why that doesn’t make him&nbsp;
a bigger suspect towards being the campus killer.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:06.160
The whole campus killer thing is honestly a&nbsp;
pretty big plot hole. Ben is playing video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:10.480
games with his friend, Asian Andy. They’re&nbsp;
using an XBOX One. I’ll excuse the use of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:14.720
console that’s 22 years old in-universe, and&nbsp;
just assume they are fans of retro games.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:18.480
What I can’t excuse, is the fact this&nbsp;
is where Asian Andy is taken out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:22.640
Ben lies about having been there. That means&nbsp;
there are no cameras in the hallways of the dorms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:26.080
even though we clearly see that there are&nbsp;
cameras on campus in the first episode.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:30.160
TURNER: “This is awful. I feel&nbsp;
bad even taking a picture.”

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:31.120
Demonetized!

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:34.800
Speaking of that first episode -- I&nbsp;
think there may be a big callback to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:38.480
the first movie. A man named Marcus finds&nbsp;
himself stranded outside during The Purge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:42.000
and tries to get his neighbors to let him&nbsp;
in, but nobody does. It seems very similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:46.400
to the situation that Dante Bishop was in in the&nbsp;
first film. That’s not the only time that Marcus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:50.480
seems to be connected to the Purge movie that&nbsp;
started it all, because his flashback scene takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:56.240
us back 14 years to 2022, the same date that&nbsp;
The Sandins had their home invaded by purgers.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:19:00.960
This is also where we meet Plus Head. Kind&nbsp;
of a minor role to justify selling masks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:04.480
but I’ll take it. Plus Head can be&nbsp;
seen rolling with his buddy: Run Mouth.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:08.560
Marcus has one more connection to the first movie&nbsp;
though. He has dinner with his wife Michelle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:11.920
in what they believe could possibly be their&nbsp;
last meal because it’s right before the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:14.880
Purge. She is playing Dubussy’s&nbsp;
Clair de Lune in the background.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:19.099
🎵 Clair de Lune - Dubussy 🎵

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:21.120
MICHELLE: "So if this is our last meal."

00:19:24.334 --> 00:19:27.291
This is the same song we heard at&nbsp;
the very beginning of the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.291 --> 00:19:30.720
which accompanied the montage of purgers&nbsp;
from previous years. Marcus and Michelle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:33.360
both have the same dinner idea in mind that night.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:40.000
MARCUS: “You did not cook. The night before Purge?”
MICHELLE: “We had sushi on our first date, remember?”&nbsp;

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.560
MARCUS: “Yeah, where do you think I&nbsp;
stopped before I came home?”

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:48.733
That one feels a little too close to the&nbsp;
anniversary shared by Nick and Amy in Gone Girl.

00:19:51.817 --> 00:19:59.560
“It’s… we had that joke that our sex was too&nbsp;
good for ordinary, so these are 2000 thread…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:05.360
We’re so cute.”

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:08.000
Marcus realizes that someone is&nbsp;
trying to purge him because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:11.280
a bounty posted on the dark web.&nbsp;
The site is called “Ivory Road”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:15.280
a parody of “The Silk Road”, which was one&nbsp;
of the first dark web drug trading sites.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:16.800
“Wait, so how do you know about this?”

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:20.880
I know about that as a fan of the internet&nbsp;
and internet history, not as a customer.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:24.800
Another important detail in this scene is the&nbsp;
calendar app seen on this phone, which is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:29.520
only two a couple details that help confirm when&nbsp;
the season takes place. We know it’s before 2037,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:34.160
because Ryan’s drivers license that expires in&nbsp;
late 2036 is still valid. But because it has to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:39.120
after 2032, based on how far back the flashbacks&nbsp;
go. That gives us a range of 4 possible years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:43.680
where it could take place, and this calendar&nbsp;
only aligns with one of those -- it’s 2036.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:46.800
Just don’t take the dates&nbsp;
of birth here too seriously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:51.440
This guy doesn’t look 55, but maybe&nbsp;
people age more gracefully in the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:55.040
And apparently the headphone jack is&nbsp;
going to make a comeback and… a tape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:59.680
recorder…? Seriously? I feel like the technology&nbsp;
problem in The Purge is never gonna get fixed.

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:03.520
But this Ivory Road account becomes something&nbsp;
that Marcus would keep coming back to in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:06.800
order to try to catch whoever ordered&nbsp;
his hit. He puts up a post of his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:10.160
to try to find someone who can get him more&nbsp;
information -- one of the responses is spam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:13.680
advertising “Cheap Boner pillz the&nbsp;
NFFA doesn’t want you to have!”

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:16.160
The final storyline follows a band of thieves and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:21.120
the NFFA agent whose computer screen&nbsp;
is home to even more Things You Missed.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:33.600
The Purge is a night where all crime&nbsp;
is legal, but for whatever reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:38.160
the first four movies and the first TV&nbsp;
season focus entirely on murder -- no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:41.520
other crime is addressed, outside of just&nbsp;
a tiny side character here and there.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:46.080
Season 2 totally changes that, making it one of&nbsp;
my favorite installments. We see bank robbery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:50.880
conspiracy, treason and hacking. That makes&nbsp;
it all the more disappointing that Season 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:54.480
is the only entry not to have a Blu-Ray&nbsp;
release. That makes it very difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:58.160
to find little details, but with that&nbsp;
all being said… here’s what I found.

00:21:58.160 --> 00:22:02.240
During the initial bank robbery scene, Tommy has&nbsp;
a shirt that says “break the rules.” He breaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:06.320
his gang’s code by going back for one more bag&nbsp;
of money as the sirens are beginning to sound.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:09.840
This is what leads to him getting arrested,&nbsp;
because he doesn’t make it out in time.

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:13.040
He should have tried to make the argument that&nbsp;
his foot was lifted off the ground. I mean how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:17.520
does this work anyway? Is it college rules&nbsp;
or NFL? We need some clarity, James Demonaco.

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:22.160
Anyway, Tommy goes to jail for 1 year before his&nbsp;
execution date on the next Purge. During his time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:27.040
in the clink, Ryan brings him a book. It’s the&nbsp;
1844 novel, The Count of Monte Cristo, about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:31.920
an innocent man who is wrongly, but deliberately&nbsp;
imprisoned. The book ends with one final message:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:37.040
all human wisdom is contained in these two&nbsp;
words, 'Wait and Hope'. This was Ryan’s way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:41.040
of telling him that if he holds on until Purge&nbsp;
night, they may have a way of breaking him out.

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:43.920
In the meantime, they also plan&nbsp;
their heist for the next Purge.

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:47.040
ZIV: “Our city attracts a lot&nbsp;
of people on Purge night.”

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:50.240
Although it hasn’t been updated in a couple&nbsp;
years now, this actually calls back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:54.240
NFFA website, that was used to promote&nbsp;
each Purge movie. In 2014 for The Purge:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:58.080
Anarchy iteration of the site, they did a&nbsp;
blog post about the top 5 Purge destinations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:02.240
where New Orleans ranked number 3 in the&nbsp;
country. I guess that held up for over a decade.

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:04.160
It’s actually referenced&nbsp;
again in the fifth episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:07.200
where Miami and New Orleans are&nbsp;
hot tourism destinations during The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:07.760
Purge, which also sets up the whole murder tourism&nbsp;
subplot in Election Year a few years later.

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:11.600
Part of Ryan’s plan for stealing the bank’s money&nbsp;
is to let the jackal’s steal the money for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:15.440
and then steal it back later in the night. The&nbsp;
jackals are thieves that steal from other thieves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:18.080
so Ryan’s plan involves giving&nbsp;
them a taste of their own medicine.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:21.440
When he and Esme look up the jackal’s&nbsp;
identities, I noticed some familiar names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:25.520
like Trent Sarreal and Sam Bogh. These&nbsp;
are also the names of Professor Adams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:29.040
test subjects from her experiment about&nbsp;
the effect of purging on the human brain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:33.360
The photos are different though… and the&nbsp;
test subjects did not survive, so I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:37.760
this is a mistake, like they accidentally&nbsp;
used the name twice for minor characters.

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:41.440
Season 2 is not void of crew reference&nbsp;
easter eggs. Ryan has a flashback to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:45.040
days on the police force, where we we the&nbsp;
locker next to his is labeled Federico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:48.320
named after second unit director&nbsp;
of photography Federico Veradi.

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:52.160
During that same flashback, Ryan catches a&nbsp;
surprise out in the field on Purge night.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:55.360
“Reynolds what the f*** are you doing out here?”

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:58.000
I already brought up Jeff Reynolds. He&nbsp;
was also a crew reference easter egg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:01.680
in Season 1. Ryan’s director of training&nbsp;
at the police academy was Amy Krebsbach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:04.560
who was named after the truck costumer.&nbsp;
There’s also an article written about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:08.320
one of Ryan’s heroic actions as an officer.&nbsp;
It’s written by James Crafton (that’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:12.480
production secretary) and there are mentions&nbsp;
of names like Paul, Emily and Raul Uddo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:16.480
all probably family members of Paul Uddo, the&nbsp;
first AD who was also mentioned in Season 1.

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:19.920
The other article shown here mentions a gas&nbsp;
station incident, where Ryan is named again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:23.280
along with his superior, Andrea Ziv.&nbsp;
Her character is named after writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:26.960
and co-producer Krystal Houghton Ziv. The&nbsp;
article mentions a couple more crew members:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:30.480
travel coordinator Callie Martin,&nbsp;
second assistant director Ben Ledoux.

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:34.080
Esme then moves onto a letter that&nbsp;
Ryan received from the mayor in 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:37.680
It’s signed by a Colleen E Moody,&nbsp;
who is also the script supervisor.

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.400
There’s one other crew reference&nbsp;
later on. Remember the part where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:42.400
Asian Andy raves about Dr. Updale’s book?

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:45.600
ANDY: “I’m halfway through Dr.&nbsp;
Updale’s Foundations of the Experiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:47.680
Unauthorized. I’m kind of a history buff.”&nbsp;

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:51.680
BONNIE: “NFFA History?”
ANDY: “Yeah. It was a best-seller.”&nbsp;

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:54.160
KELLEN:“Didn’t she kill herself after&nbsp;
creating the first purge night?”&nbsp;

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:57.280
ANDY: “No, that’s been&nbsp;
debunked. She died Purging.”

00:24:57.280 --> 00:25:00.800
Not only does that explain exactly what&nbsp;
the NFFA told the public after the events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.800 --> 00:25:04.640
of The First Purge, but we see this book&nbsp;
throughout the series. First on a poster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:07.840
then Parker finds the book itself in&nbsp;
Ben’s possession, where we see the author:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.840 --> 00:25:10.960
M Martin, a reference to the&nbsp;
prop master Michael S Martin.

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:14.800
There’s also a poster that can be spotted&nbsp;
in town advertising for Purge: The Musical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:19.520
a story of good vs evil. I wonder if that’s&nbsp;
a sign of things to come. That’s right. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:23.200
heard it here first. The Forever&nbsp;
Purge is going to be a musical.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:28.080
RYAN’S MOM: “Why aren’t you in school?”
RYAN: “It got cancelled.”

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:30.320
Well that one hit home a little bit HARD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:32.800
Not the first time the Purge&nbsp;
has predicted a disaster either.

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:34.960
When I did the Things You Missed&nbsp;
episode of The First Purge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:37.040
I pointed out this wall art of a boxer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:40.560
and explained how it represents the character&nbsp;
Nya. In the Frat House where Ben tortures his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:44.480
fellow fraternity members there’s a portrait&nbsp;
of a football player in a very similar style.

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:48.000
Another callback can be seen when Ryan’s&nbsp;
gang busts Tommy out of captivity. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.960
seems the Mercy Cult bus from&nbsp;
Season 1 has been repurposed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:54.000
and is now used as the prisoner&nbsp;
transport vehicle on Purge night.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:58.800
Season 2 comes to a close as Ben truly&nbsp;
becomes the new Godhead during the 2037&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:02.880
purge. He takes out most of his frat, but&nbsp;
Parker survives. As Ben is hunting him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.520
he’s hit by a car, and&nbsp;
wakes up in a triage center.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:09.040
When he comes to, the first thing he sees&nbsp;
is a Jesus statue looking down on him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:12.080
Perhaps this angers him, because&nbsp;
he now sees himself as God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:14.640
he doesn’t want to be looked&nbsp;
down on by this religious figure.

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:27.200
BEN: “You’re just trying to play&nbsp;
God. But you’re not God. I AM!”

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:31.440
Marcus is able to sedate him and save the&nbsp;
rest of the Triage Center, but Ben survives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:35.520
In the final shots, he can be seen preparing&nbsp;
for another Purge. It’s an interesting choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:39.280
and if my theory is correct, that means&nbsp;
both major villains from the TV series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:42.320
the new Godhead and Joe Owens, both survive.

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:46.800
As of now, The Purge TV series is cancelled, and&nbsp;
the upcoming movie, The Forever Purge is supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:51.520
to be the ending of the series. How cool would it&nbsp;
be to see Joe and Ben, two of the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:56.240
Purge villains we’ve seen so far, team up as&nbsp;
secondary antagonists in the franchise finale?

00:26:56.240 --> 00:27:00.000
Whatever happens, I’ll be sure to cover The&nbsp;
Forever Purge here on Things You Missed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.320
So make sure you don’t miss it by subscribing&nbsp;
to CZsWorld for new horrors every week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:09.840
ring the deathbell for all notifications and&nbsp;
I’ll see you in the next one. Assuming we both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:15.309
survive. If you don’t want to get purged stay&nbsp;
home, but if you have to go out, wear a mask.

